WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.700
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.702 --> 00:00:08.160
MARK KUBIK: We're
going to take you

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.320
along a little bit
of our journey today,

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.540
of getting to the cloud
and building applications

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:15.390
in the cloud at Global
Payments, and how

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:17.940
we're using Looker and
BigQuery to scale up

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:22.037
around the world for an
application that we've built.

00:00:22.037 --> 00:00:23.120
Just a little background--

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:25.200
Global Payments, a
large payment processor,

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.260
headquartered in Atlanta,
Georgia, $4 billion

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:29.460
in net revenue last year.

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:35.190
If you don't know much about
us, about 2.5 million customers

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:37.950
that we've given some
sort of payment solution

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:39.670
to around the world.

00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:44.170
And about 11,000 employees,
is the size of our company.

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:46.920
We have a strategy
at Global Payments.

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:50.790
It's about software
driven, partnered solutions

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:51.690
around the world.

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:53.460
An Omni channel
experience, you've

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:55.702
seen that in some of the
presentations around here--

00:00:55.702 --> 00:00:57.660
we want to make sure that
we can deliver things

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:00.240
across multiple channels
around the world

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:02.947
and get into faster
growth markets.

00:01:02.947 --> 00:01:05.280
One of the ways that we're
going to expand those markets

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:07.650
is going to the cloud.

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:12.180
Probably about a couple of years
ago, our Chief Product Officer,

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:13.410
Frank Young, had a mission.

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:18.240
He wanted to create a best in
class engagement platform--

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.650
not a BI platform, not
just a reporting platform,

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:25.980
but something that attracted
customers, created stickiness,

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:28.710
and was something that we could
be proud of at Global Payments.

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:30.870
So he partnered with us.

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:34.440
And about that same
time, our CIO Guido Saki,

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:37.085
put us on a cloud first
strategy at Global Payments.

00:01:37.085 --> 00:01:39.210
He knew that we needed to
have a multi cloud, cloud

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:42.880
first strategy to deliver
things around the world.

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:45.010
We had to have reduced
total cost of ownership.

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:46.260
We couldn't do things on prem.

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:48.690
You've heard that theory
many different places.

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:51.450
So, he launched us
on that strategy.

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:53.910
We needed to build the
technology solutions

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:57.210
that our customers would
like around the world.

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:01.080
We needed a platform that we
could innovate on and deliver

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:05.926
new products every year, every
quarter, to drive business.

00:02:05.926 --> 00:02:08.259
And we needed to do it securely
and safely in the cloud.

00:02:08.259 --> 00:02:10.759
You've heard a lot of things
about security in Google Cloud,

00:02:10.759 --> 00:02:12.720
but those were really
the driving factors

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:16.727
that launched us on this
journey that we've been on.

00:02:16.727 --> 00:02:18.810
I don't know where all of
you are in your journey,

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:20.450
so I'll review a little bit.

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:21.700
This is a couple of years ago.

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:25.312
We started to take a look
at different vendors,

00:02:25.312 --> 00:02:26.020
different things.

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:28.740
We enlisted the help of our
Architecture Department,

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:31.950
led by Ali Fawaz, to look
at some of these factors

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:33.385
to help us out.

00:02:33.385 --> 00:02:36.010
Probably you guys had these same
factors, but I'll review them.

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:38.580
We needed not just a BI tool--

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:41.520
everybody seems to have
one, two, three of them.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:43.537
BI tools network for
the mass markets.

00:02:43.537 --> 00:02:45.120
We needed something
that could provide

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:49.470
embedded BI in that engagement
platform we're talking about.

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:51.450
We needed RESTful API enables.

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:54.870
Everybody out here is probably
trying to produce APIs,

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:58.500
trying to get out there where
people can code to your API,

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:01.380
rather than us have to
code for them out there.

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:04.140
We needed something that
was responsive design.

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:06.120
Everybody in here has
a different device,

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:08.070
a different mode of connecting.

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:10.440
We needed to make
sure that platform

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:13.890
was able to be
displayed on any device,

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:17.190
visually appealing to anybody
that wanted to do business

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:18.570
with Global Payments.

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:20.640
We needed a powerful
abstraction layer,

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:22.170
so that when we
changed data, that

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:24.568
changes very readily
out there, we

00:03:24.568 --> 00:03:25.860
could put something in between.

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:28.380
That's where we used
the Looker platform.

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:30.570
And we'll get to
that later in there.

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:33.120
And we needed to
integrate with the cloud.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:37.170
We have all evaluated many
different cloud vendors.

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:39.400
We chose a multiple cloud
strategy, we got that.

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:43.320
But for this application, we
chose Google Cloud Platform.

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:45.390
We needed something that
could power a data lake.

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:46.110
Data's King.

00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:49.140
You got to have data to
do anything these days.

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:51.180
We evaluated things
on the technology now

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:53.610
and where we thought things
were going in the future.

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:55.193
You have to look
forward a little bit.

00:03:55.193 --> 00:03:58.470
Things change very fast
out there every day.

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:02.250
The total cost of
ownership was a big factor

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:05.370
for how we wanted to produce
our applications in the cloud.

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:06.420
Security is number one.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:07.860
We're a payment processor.

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:09.870
If we don't keep your
credit card number safe

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:12.390
and all your personal data
safe, we're out of business.

00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:16.285
So we needed to use that
as a guiding principle.

00:04:16.285 --> 00:04:18.410
And a data center region
availability-- everybody's

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:20.190
heard of GDPR.

00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:22.410
India's put some
rules in lately,

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:24.930
to where you may have to
put your application locally

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:26.440
you may have put
your data locally.

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:28.830
So forward thinking,
this has allowed

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:32.850
us to take some of those
factors into account.

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:37.200
Why we chose Google Cloud
Platform and Looker out there--

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:39.990
again, GCP, Looker,
BigQuery and App Engine

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:41.440
are the main components.

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:43.320
Jason's going to walk
you through the design

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:46.710
and the pieces we used
to get to the platform

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:48.360
that we're rolling out.

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:50.680
We needed a customer
first design.

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:53.400
One of the things that
we learned over time

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:55.330
was you must listen
to the customer.

00:04:55.330 --> 00:04:57.060
You've guys have all
probably heard it.

00:04:57.060 --> 00:05:01.380
But we actually used My
Team plus some partners,

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:04.740
to build a prototype
almost, two years ago,

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:07.300
that we launched out
to the customers.

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:09.120
We got feedback back.

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:12.750
In January of '18, our design
team, led by Clare Bowden,

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:14.790
out of Ireland,
redesigned this thing,

00:05:14.790 --> 00:05:16.230
listened to the customer.

00:05:16.230 --> 00:05:18.810
In January of '18, we really
started building this out

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:20.010
for the customers.

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:21.920
We went to beta in the summer.

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:24.482
And we have been getting
feedback from the customers.

00:05:24.482 --> 00:05:26.940
And I'll tell you where our
journey is gone to a little bit

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:28.080
later.

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:29.760
We needed API enabled.

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:33.700
Again, if we cannot enable
an API for our customers,

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:36.750
because we serve everybody
from the small mom and pop,

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:39.010
to the medium sized guy
with four franchises,

00:05:39.010 --> 00:05:40.458
to the Verizons of the world.

00:05:40.458 --> 00:05:42.750
So this architecture that
Jason is going to talk about,

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:44.820
services all of those customers.

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:46.540
Again, any device, anywhere.

00:05:46.540 --> 00:05:48.840
And then we want to make
sure that we could launch

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:50.700
on an analytics platform--

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:53.400
Google BigQuery, and a lot
of the things that GCP,

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:56.370
that you're hearing around all
these sessions, could provide,

00:05:56.370 --> 00:05:58.230
and we knew would
enhance our offering

00:05:58.230 --> 00:06:01.310
to all of our customers.

00:06:01.310 --> 00:06:03.560
Our custom built application
looks a little like this.

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:05.600
And I'll go into it
a little bit more

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:07.850
after Jason gives
you a little more

00:06:07.850 --> 00:06:10.550
of the nuts and bolts
of the architecture.

00:06:10.550 --> 00:06:12.650
But we need to scale
to any enterprise.

00:06:12.650 --> 00:06:14.540
We need to scale
to-- your mom and dad

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:17.360
may have their small business,
it needs to be useful for them.

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:19.818
It needs to be useful for the
medium guy and the large guy.

00:06:19.818 --> 00:06:22.790
It needs to be extensible
to any platform and highly

00:06:22.790 --> 00:06:26.100
customizable, because it
has to be one-size-fits-all

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:29.250
for our users.

00:06:29.250 --> 00:06:31.470
What I'd like to show
you now is, if you

00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:34.835
could switch to demo one, is--

00:06:38.470 --> 00:06:40.170
hold on.

00:06:40.170 --> 00:06:41.430
This is where demo--

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:48.710
there we go.

00:06:48.710 --> 00:06:50.260
Can you switch to the demo one?

00:06:50.260 --> 00:06:51.590
There you go.

00:06:51.590 --> 00:06:54.280
This is a little marketing video
that our team has put together,

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:56.021
the portal that we're launching.

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:07.650
Built on top of GCP,
with the components

00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:09.360
that Jason's going to describe.

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:11.400
And I'll let him get up
here and talk about it.

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:15.757
But this is a portal designed
for everybody around the world

00:07:15.757 --> 00:07:17.340
that we do business
with today, to get

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:19.560
the information about
the transactions we

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:23.050
process for them.

00:07:23.050 --> 00:07:24.070
Looks pretty slick.

00:07:24.070 --> 00:07:25.653
In my opinion, I
think our design team

00:07:25.653 --> 00:07:26.633
has done a great job.

00:07:26.633 --> 00:07:28.300
But I'll let Jason
tell you a little bit

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:30.660
about how we built that.

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:33.130
JASON DERLEIN:
Cool, thanks, Mark.

00:07:33.130 --> 00:07:36.000
So like Mark said, we've kind
of went on this journey probably

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:36.900
about two years ago.

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:40.140
We started-- we
kind of got the--

00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:43.740
we decided we're going to go
all-in on the cloud, which

00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:45.090
is a pretty big change for us.

00:07:45.090 --> 00:07:49.760
And can you guys
go back to slides?

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:51.915
There we go, we're good.

00:07:51.915 --> 00:07:53.290
So I'm going to
talk a little bit

00:07:53.290 --> 00:07:55.150
on what we actually built here.

00:07:55.150 --> 00:07:59.010
So, obviously, it's a
modern web application.

00:07:59.010 --> 00:08:01.650
It's designed
completely in React.

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:05.040
Everything's hosted in GCP.

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:07.290
We went with a
microservice architecture,

00:08:07.290 --> 00:08:08.880
as everyone is doing nowadays.

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:11.608
And that gives us
some extensibility.

00:08:11.608 --> 00:08:13.150
The application does
a lot of things.

00:08:13.150 --> 00:08:15.317
But I think the most important
thing that we're here

00:08:15.317 --> 00:08:19.200
to talk about today is how we're
getting this data from our data

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:20.790
lake to the customers.

00:08:20.790 --> 00:08:22.650
And it's a little
nonconventional here,

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:24.090
but what we're
actually going with

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:27.990
is streaming this data
from BigQuery itself

00:08:27.990 --> 00:08:30.300
to the customer in real time.

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:32.900
There's no reporting
layer in between.

00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:34.549
We're not doing any
pre-aggregations

00:08:34.549 --> 00:08:37.049
that you would typically see
with like a Lambda architecture

00:08:37.049 --> 00:08:39.450
or something like that.

00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:43.565
So, pretty standard
web application.

00:08:43.565 --> 00:08:45.690
What I want to focus on
today is the reporting API,

00:08:45.690 --> 00:08:48.210
which is how we're
actually getting this down.

00:08:48.210 --> 00:08:51.670
As Mark alluded to, BigQuery
and Looker are our keys to that.

00:08:51.670 --> 00:08:56.430
So let me start with what
our goals were for this.

00:08:56.430 --> 00:08:58.680
So, we have some
disparate data sources.

00:08:58.680 --> 00:08:59.853
Some things come in batch.

00:08:59.853 --> 00:09:02.020
At the end the day, I'm
sure a lot of you have that.

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:05.002
We also have some stuff
that comes in real time.

00:09:05.002 --> 00:09:06.210
So there's a challenge there.

00:09:06.210 --> 00:09:09.500
You can't go with kind of like
a batch layer, where you process

00:09:09.500 --> 00:09:13.530
it nightly, because we have
streaming data coming in

00:09:13.530 --> 00:09:14.920
at the same time.

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:17.273
So obviously,
that's a challenge.

00:09:17.273 --> 00:09:18.690
There's been
solutions to do that.

00:09:18.690 --> 00:09:20.025
I don't know if you guys heard
of the Lambda architecture,

00:09:20.025 --> 00:09:21.520
where you're take
your batch layer,

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:23.520
you'll take you're
streaming, and you'll kind of

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:27.510
amalgamate them in an API
layer and serve that up

00:09:27.510 --> 00:09:28.590
to the customer.

00:09:28.590 --> 00:09:31.270
That was one option.

00:09:31.270 --> 00:09:34.830
The other thing we need to do
is, we want some self-service.

00:09:34.830 --> 00:09:38.040
In previous lives
of this application,

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:41.670
we found ourselves having lots
and lots of different reports

00:09:41.670 --> 00:09:44.603
that our team would have
to customize and design.

00:09:44.603 --> 00:09:46.770
They're really just
permutations around other stuff.

00:09:46.770 --> 00:09:50.190
So we would love to give some
self-service to the customer

00:09:50.190 --> 00:09:55.280
and let them say I want this
column, I want that column.

00:09:55.280 --> 00:09:57.540
And you'll see
where that comes in.

00:09:57.540 --> 00:10:01.020
The third thing is, we
want it relatively fast.

00:10:01.020 --> 00:10:03.480
This will-- you'll
see as we get in,

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:06.210
this is the biggest challenge
to this architecture,

00:10:06.210 --> 00:10:08.400
going directly to BigQuery
is getting that speed

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:11.670
to web level, web speed.

00:10:11.670 --> 00:10:13.320
And then, obviously,
fault tolerance--

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:16.020
when you're going through and
you're building a data lake,

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:17.560
you really don't want to--

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:19.380
you want to be able
to start from scratch.

00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:21.900
You want to be able to
go back in and rebuild

00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:23.460
the entire thing.

00:10:23.460 --> 00:10:26.210
It's kind of the mindset
of the whole MapReduce.

00:10:26.210 --> 00:10:27.840
You have the data
lake and you want

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:30.590
to be able to rebuild that from
scratch if your design changes,

00:10:30.590 --> 00:10:33.240
say aggregates change,
anything like that.

00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:35.490
I need to be able to go
back and start from scratch,

00:10:35.490 --> 00:10:38.370
and not have, basically,
an immutable data

00:10:38.370 --> 00:10:40.950
set at the end of the day.

00:10:40.950 --> 00:10:42.750
So with those goals,
what we've ended up

00:10:42.750 --> 00:10:47.250
building is a reporting
framework that kind of lives

00:10:47.250 --> 00:10:49.920
on what we're calling a
multilayered data lake.

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:54.420
So, you can start with
the regular batch data,

00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:56.760
comes in, files, we're
throwing all that

00:10:56.760 --> 00:11:00.090
into GCP storage buckets.

00:11:00.090 --> 00:11:03.060
From there, we move on--
we essentially move it

00:11:03.060 --> 00:11:06.978
as is to BigQuery, one for one.

00:11:06.978 --> 00:11:08.520
We're calling that
our trusted layer.

00:11:08.520 --> 00:11:10.020
This is kind of our
Global Payments.

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:11.640
These are not industry
standard terms.

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:14.700
This is what internally we
are calling these layers.

00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:16.980
So the trusted layer is
pretty much straight in from--

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:23.690
one for one, like for
like from the data set.

00:11:23.690 --> 00:11:26.200
And then we'll go through and
we'll put some metadata on it.

00:11:26.200 --> 00:11:27.890
We'll protect sensitive data.

00:11:27.890 --> 00:11:29.300
We're not going
to persist things

00:11:29.300 --> 00:11:31.247
that shouldn't be persisted.

00:11:31.247 --> 00:11:33.080
At that point, we move
it to a second layer,

00:11:33.080 --> 00:11:34.250
which is also in BigQuery.

00:11:34.250 --> 00:11:36.947
So these are BigQuery
to BigQuery jobs.

00:11:36.947 --> 00:11:39.030
And that's what we're
calling the transform layer.

00:11:39.030 --> 00:11:40.880
So this is where we're going
to put some data quality

00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:41.390
rules out.

00:11:41.390 --> 00:11:44.000
We're going to go ahead
and normalize things

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:50.000
like dates, data types, get that
data in a clean state, where

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:51.930
you can put other
tools on top of it,

00:11:51.930 --> 00:11:55.820
whether it be eventually AI
and MEL, that will come off

00:11:55.820 --> 00:11:56.690
the transform layer.

00:11:56.690 --> 00:11:59.930
This is our normalized
kind of data quality.

00:11:59.930 --> 00:12:02.750
Now, I can always go back and
rebuild that from the bucket,

00:12:02.750 --> 00:12:05.480
to the trusted, to
the transformed.

00:12:05.480 --> 00:12:08.500
The key, I think, to this
design though is really,

00:12:08.500 --> 00:12:10.250
we're working on the
consumption layer.

00:12:10.250 --> 00:12:13.070
And this is kind of
equivalent to a batch

00:12:13.070 --> 00:12:18.115
layer in a more traditional
big data architecture.

00:12:18.115 --> 00:12:19.240
All the consumption layer--

00:12:19.240 --> 00:12:20.865
I think of the
consumption layer almost

00:12:20.865 --> 00:12:22.440
like a materialized view.

00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:24.740
This is where we can take
multiple data sources that

00:12:24.740 --> 00:12:26.860
came in from the
transform layer,

00:12:26.860 --> 00:12:30.740
aggregate them together, flatten
them out, run metrics on it.

00:12:30.740 --> 00:12:33.320
If we have some common
KPIs or any aggregations

00:12:33.320 --> 00:12:35.750
that we want to run, we put
them in the consumption layer.

00:12:39.720 --> 00:12:43.530
The problem is, then how do you
get that to the application?

00:12:43.530 --> 00:12:45.540
I really don't want
all my applications

00:12:45.540 --> 00:12:49.260
writing directly to BigQuery,
especially from the internet.

00:12:49.260 --> 00:12:51.190
So this is where we have
our consumer system,

00:12:51.190 --> 00:12:53.860
which, as you can see up
there, this is Looker.

00:12:53.860 --> 00:12:57.090
And what we're using Looker
as is really that abstraction

00:12:57.090 --> 00:13:01.590
layer to our data lake.

00:13:01.590 --> 00:13:03.300
Got a little slide
here on Looker.

00:13:03.300 --> 00:13:05.257
Looker's given us
all a lot of stuff.

00:13:05.257 --> 00:13:06.590
Is anybody familiar with Looker?

00:13:06.590 --> 00:13:11.870
If you're not, the platform
itself is really just--

00:13:11.870 --> 00:13:15.020
it's its own modeling
language, really XML based,

00:13:15.020 --> 00:13:17.510
where you can basically
define a contract,

00:13:17.510 --> 00:13:19.590
and put the data
behind the scenes.

00:13:19.590 --> 00:13:22.550
And Looker will go ahead and
form the query up and return

00:13:22.550 --> 00:13:24.433
the data back to you.

00:13:24.433 --> 00:13:25.850
This helped us a
lot, because when

00:13:25.850 --> 00:13:27.267
we were building
this application,

00:13:27.267 --> 00:13:29.605
we also came at it
from two prongs.

00:13:29.605 --> 00:13:31.730
We were building the web
application, the reporting

00:13:31.730 --> 00:13:35.390
layer, and the data
warehouse, the data lake, all

00:13:35.390 --> 00:13:37.210
those layers, at the same time.

00:13:37.210 --> 00:13:38.960
So what we did with
Looker is we basically

00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:41.120
used Looker as a contract.

00:13:41.120 --> 00:13:43.400
The model that we
defined in Looker,

00:13:43.400 --> 00:13:45.320
we might have stubbed
some of it out.

00:13:45.320 --> 00:13:47.750
Now the front end team,
who's building the APIs

00:13:47.750 --> 00:13:50.930
can start coding against
that, while the back end ETL

00:13:50.930 --> 00:13:52.630
teams are actually
fulfilling the data

00:13:52.630 --> 00:13:54.463
sets behind the scenes.

00:13:54.463 --> 00:13:55.880
And if we need to
change anything,

00:13:55.880 --> 00:13:58.160
we change it at that one place.

00:13:58.160 --> 00:14:00.560
That kind of gets to the
idea of the consistent,

00:14:00.560 --> 00:14:02.590
no matter who's
looking into this data,

00:14:02.590 --> 00:14:04.240
if it's going through
our Looker model,

00:14:04.240 --> 00:14:06.610
everyone's going to get
it exactly the same.

00:14:06.610 --> 00:14:11.290
Looker also offers an API, which
is key to this kind of design.

00:14:11.290 --> 00:14:15.278
So, they have a very
robust API, where

00:14:15.278 --> 00:14:17.570
I can do filtering, column
selection, things like that.

00:14:17.570 --> 00:14:20.150
Anything that's in my
model, I can essentially

00:14:20.150 --> 00:14:21.200
query through the API.

00:14:21.200 --> 00:14:23.690
They almost have an
API first mentality.

00:14:23.690 --> 00:14:26.630
So, I don't have to write
SQL for my code anymore.

00:14:26.630 --> 00:14:31.940
I can use Looker just
like another data API.

00:14:31.940 --> 00:14:35.960
It just happens to have
BigQuery behind the scenes.

00:14:35.960 --> 00:14:39.070
Looker also gives
us visualizations.

00:14:39.070 --> 00:14:40.920
So there are embedded
visualizations.

00:14:40.920 --> 00:14:42.460
You see on the
screen right there,

00:14:42.460 --> 00:14:44.945
you have the little
charts and graphs.

00:14:44.945 --> 00:14:46.320
Those are all
driven from Looker,

00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:50.160
from the exact same model
that we get the data from,

00:14:50.160 --> 00:14:52.338
which is--

00:14:52.338 --> 00:14:54.130
it really helps in the
development efforts.

00:14:54.130 --> 00:14:56.490
I don't have to do
any extra development

00:14:56.490 --> 00:15:00.450
to get a pie chart around the
same data that I already have.

00:15:00.450 --> 00:15:03.840
I just basically configure
what called a Look.

00:15:03.840 --> 00:15:04.500
I call that.

00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:06.253
I can embed that
in my application.

00:15:06.253 --> 00:15:07.170
You'll see that later.

00:15:07.170 --> 00:15:09.710
Mark's going to show a
little bit more of the app.

00:15:09.710 --> 00:15:12.367
Paging, that's another
thing where we do directly

00:15:12.367 --> 00:15:13.200
in the Looker model.

00:15:13.200 --> 00:15:16.920
So, some of this data is huge.

00:15:16.920 --> 00:15:18.780
We're not going to
serve up through an API

00:15:18.780 --> 00:15:20.700
10 million records coming back.

00:15:20.700 --> 00:15:22.380
So I can actually get
paging, and Looker

00:15:22.380 --> 00:15:25.740
takes care of doing the limits
for me, behind the scenes.

00:15:25.740 --> 00:15:27.600
It's completely
abstracted from me.

00:15:27.600 --> 00:15:31.050
I can just say, here's the
data I want, I want page six.

00:15:31.050 --> 00:15:34.330
It'll tell me how
many come back.

00:15:34.330 --> 00:15:35.880
The other key for us was export.

00:15:35.880 --> 00:15:39.050
One of our requirements
is some of our customers

00:15:39.050 --> 00:15:42.590
like to take this data
from the portal, put it

00:15:42.590 --> 00:15:45.520
into their own
accounting system.

00:15:45.520 --> 00:15:47.960
So rather than have to go
through and write an export

00:15:47.960 --> 00:15:50.330
system to do this, there
is export functionalities

00:15:50.330 --> 00:15:52.683
built in, in Looker we
essentially just proxy

00:15:52.683 --> 00:15:53.600
them straight through.

00:15:56.705 --> 00:15:58.830
So let me talk a little
bit about what we've built.

00:15:58.830 --> 00:16:01.880
I might have to use the mouse
here to point to some of this.

00:16:01.880 --> 00:16:07.300
So what we really did here
was, we built an API layer

00:16:07.300 --> 00:16:09.150
on top of the Looker API.

00:16:09.150 --> 00:16:12.490
We kind of extended
it a little bit.

00:16:12.490 --> 00:16:14.000
It served a couple of purposes.

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:17.320
The first thing was, our
application is a SPA,

00:16:17.320 --> 00:16:18.460
it's a react application.

00:16:18.460 --> 00:16:20.590
It uses JWTs.

00:16:20.590 --> 00:16:22.120
Obviously, I have
to have something

00:16:22.120 --> 00:16:24.070
to convert that JWT into Looker.

00:16:24.070 --> 00:16:27.820
So our custom API that
proxies over to Looker

00:16:27.820 --> 00:16:30.990
is handling the
security from the JWT,

00:16:30.990 --> 00:16:33.587
turning it into
the Looker model.

00:16:33.587 --> 00:16:35.170
But the other thing
that we did that I

00:16:35.170 --> 00:16:40.060
think is the key to this, is we
built a little metadata engine

00:16:40.060 --> 00:16:42.100
on top of Looker.

00:16:42.100 --> 00:16:44.170
So if you see the
API layer in here,

00:16:44.170 --> 00:16:46.510
basically what
happens is just a--

00:16:46.510 --> 00:16:51.340
find my mouse, there we go.

00:16:51.340 --> 00:16:52.480
Can you guys see that?

00:16:52.480 --> 00:16:53.020
OK.

00:16:53.020 --> 00:16:58.900
So our app is really just
HTML5 react, piled down,

00:16:58.900 --> 00:17:03.040
put in a storage bucket, load
balanced through cloud load

00:17:03.040 --> 00:17:04.329
balancing.

00:17:04.329 --> 00:17:07.930
Boom, there's your
HTML5 web application.

00:17:07.930 --> 00:17:09.819
All the magic happens
here in the API layer.

00:17:09.819 --> 00:17:12.599
So pretty much the
java script from here

00:17:12.599 --> 00:17:16.660
is making Ajax calls directly
to the various microservices.

00:17:16.660 --> 00:17:18.040
The one we really
care about here

00:17:18.040 --> 00:17:21.770
is the reporting API right here.

00:17:21.770 --> 00:17:26.020
So what this guy does is,
we've persisted the metadata

00:17:26.020 --> 00:17:29.620
around the Looker
model into Cloud SQL.

00:17:29.620 --> 00:17:32.020
So this guy can
actually go look up

00:17:32.020 --> 00:17:36.880
all of the different available
columns in Cloud SQL for you.

00:17:36.880 --> 00:17:39.520
Because I know who you
are from the token,

00:17:39.520 --> 00:17:44.170
I can go say what queries,
what reports do you want.

00:17:44.170 --> 00:17:46.240
And essentially, that's
just some custom storage

00:17:46.240 --> 00:17:47.170
that we've done.

00:17:47.170 --> 00:17:49.660
And then this guy just runs
a builder pattern and proxies

00:17:49.660 --> 00:17:53.830
over whatever that request
was to the Looker API, which,

00:17:53.830 --> 00:17:57.820
in turn, generates
the BigQuery SQL,

00:17:57.820 --> 00:18:00.130
returns it back, proxies
it back to the front end,

00:18:00.130 --> 00:18:03.340
into a standard JSON format.

00:18:03.340 --> 00:18:05.080
This is really--
anybody familiar

00:18:05.080 --> 00:18:06.920
with the open/closed principle?

00:18:06.920 --> 00:18:11.410
What this has done, essentially,
has allowed us to extend this

00:18:11.410 --> 00:18:13.360
with really writing no code.

00:18:13.360 --> 00:18:15.430
I can build another
Looker model.

00:18:15.430 --> 00:18:17.620
I can put it into our
configuration database.

00:18:17.620 --> 00:18:19.510
That could point
to any data I want

00:18:19.510 --> 00:18:23.020
and expose it through this
application, through this API,

00:18:23.020 --> 00:18:24.910
with a configuration only.

00:18:24.910 --> 00:18:28.390
I have to write the Look ML,
which is the abstraction layer.

00:18:28.390 --> 00:18:29.920
Obviously, I have
to have the data.

00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:32.050
But I don't have to
do any additional code

00:18:32.050 --> 00:18:34.600
besides enabling it
in my metadata layer

00:18:34.600 --> 00:18:37.550
for the customer to get it
or the portal to get it.

00:18:37.550 --> 00:18:40.330
So now as we extend, and you
go from those 100 reports,

00:18:40.330 --> 00:18:44.110
if I end up with a new request,
I can build a new Looker model.

00:18:44.110 --> 00:18:45.700
I can put it into the app--

00:18:45.700 --> 00:18:48.970
and the security is actually
put on the model layer.

00:18:48.970 --> 00:18:52.510
So you might see a little
layer, the Report Builder,

00:18:52.510 --> 00:18:55.270
which kind of gives us
some self-service as well.

00:18:55.270 --> 00:18:58.000
So, we actually allow folks
to come in and customize what

00:18:58.000 --> 00:18:59.840
they want this to look like.

00:18:59.840 --> 00:19:02.620
So they can go into our
web API using the metadata,

00:19:02.620 --> 00:19:06.130
and say, there's 700
columns available here,

00:19:06.130 --> 00:19:08.780
I care about these six, and
I want to sort by these,

00:19:08.780 --> 00:19:10.030
and I want to filter by these.

00:19:10.030 --> 00:19:12.390
We'll persist that in Cloud SQL.

00:19:12.390 --> 00:19:13.810
And essentially,
that just builds

00:19:13.810 --> 00:19:15.150
a-- it's a builder pattern.

00:19:15.150 --> 00:19:16.930
It's just a straight
proxy to Looker.

00:19:16.930 --> 00:19:19.218
So as long as we have
the Looker model,

00:19:19.218 --> 00:19:21.760
I don't even want to know how
many permutations we could get,

00:19:21.760 --> 00:19:22.780
but it's flexible.

00:19:22.780 --> 00:19:24.340
We provide some out
of the box stuff,

00:19:24.340 --> 00:19:27.280
but the customer can actually
take the out of the box,

00:19:27.280 --> 00:19:28.870
they can modify it,
they can save it.

00:19:28.870 --> 00:19:30.580
They can share it
with their co-workers,

00:19:30.580 --> 00:19:32.860
they can share it
with other people.

00:19:32.860 --> 00:19:36.592
And that's all facilitated
from the reporting layer.

00:19:36.592 --> 00:19:38.050
The other big thing
that I think we

00:19:38.050 --> 00:19:41.020
need to talk about here is-
the title of the session

00:19:41.020 --> 00:19:43.350
is how we scale this.

00:19:43.350 --> 00:19:46.170
Everything in here is
completely scalable through GCP.

00:19:46.170 --> 00:19:49.770
So the API layers all live
in an App Engine right now.

00:19:49.770 --> 00:19:51.720
We may move that to GKE.

00:19:51.720 --> 00:19:53.910
That seems like the
logical progression.

00:19:53.910 --> 00:19:56.300
When we started this,
that wasn't a thing.

00:19:56.300 --> 00:19:59.720
Looker is actually-- we're
not hosted by Looker.

00:19:59.720 --> 00:20:03.630
We're self-hosting Looker
inside auto scale VMs.

00:20:03.630 --> 00:20:05.670
So we can expand that
out as much as we want.

00:20:05.670 --> 00:20:08.680
And obviously,
BigQuery is BigQuery.

00:20:08.680 --> 00:20:12.037
You can do as much as
you want with that.

00:20:12.037 --> 00:20:13.745
There's a couple of
pricing models on it.

00:20:13.745 --> 00:20:16.840
There's fixed cost and
then there's pay as you go.

00:20:16.840 --> 00:20:19.471
We're on a fixed cost for that.

00:20:19.471 --> 00:20:20.820
Is there anything else?

00:20:20.820 --> 00:20:21.610
Security.

00:20:21.610 --> 00:20:25.620
So, one of the challenges too
that we really needed to do

00:20:25.620 --> 00:20:27.970
is, the goal here
is have one API.

00:20:27.970 --> 00:20:33.300
And based upon your token,
I only return your data.

00:20:33.300 --> 00:20:35.802
So we have all of
the data in here.

00:20:35.802 --> 00:20:38.010
The personas that use this
application are everything

00:20:38.010 --> 00:20:40.350
from that single
merchant, who might have

00:20:40.350 --> 00:20:43.440
one location, to maybe
a regional manager that

00:20:43.440 --> 00:20:46.950
has 100 locations, to maybe
our own internal customer

00:20:46.950 --> 00:20:50.010
support people, that service
the entire portfolio.

00:20:50.010 --> 00:20:53.670
So we really wanted to ensure
that the security trimming was

00:20:53.670 --> 00:20:55.300
done in the data layer.

00:20:55.300 --> 00:20:59.130
So what we're actually
doing is, as we provision

00:20:59.130 --> 00:21:02.670
users in this application,
we're using the Cloud Pub/Sub

00:21:02.670 --> 00:21:05.790
and we're actually pushing
the data access ID.

00:21:05.790 --> 00:21:07.130
So it's just location IDs.

00:21:07.130 --> 00:21:08.880
We call them merchant
IDs, but think of it

00:21:08.880 --> 00:21:10.610
like individual locations.

00:21:10.610 --> 00:21:12.270
And we push them to BigQuery.

00:21:12.270 --> 00:21:16.890
And in Looker itself, we join
to the table [INAUDIBLE] is

00:21:16.890 --> 00:21:20.850
to the point where it can
handle someone having 200,000--

00:21:20.850 --> 00:21:23.580
a customer support person might
have access to 200,000-300,000

00:21:23.580 --> 00:21:24.477
locations.

00:21:24.477 --> 00:21:26.560
We actually security trim
that right in the layer.

00:21:26.560 --> 00:21:29.340
So when you pass over
the Looker query,

00:21:29.340 --> 00:21:32.410
you're only getting back your
data, which is fantastic.

00:21:32.410 --> 00:21:34.350
We don't have to manage
that at the API layer.

00:21:34.350 --> 00:21:37.020
We keep track of it,
we Pub/Sub it out,

00:21:37.020 --> 00:21:38.550
and we persist it to BigQuery.

00:21:38.550 --> 00:21:41.040
And we do a join on the
data to right there get

00:21:41.040 --> 00:21:44.487
the constraints, which is--

00:21:44.487 --> 00:21:46.320
I don't know if anybody's
ever experienced--

00:21:46.320 --> 00:21:47.688
that's a huge problem solved.

00:21:47.688 --> 00:21:49.230
BigQuery's been a
lifesaver for that.

00:21:49.230 --> 00:21:51.480
Most of our relational
systems could not

00:21:51.480 --> 00:21:54.390
handle joining on
something that big.

00:21:57.710 --> 00:22:00.470
So Mark talked about
the Omni channel.

00:22:00.470 --> 00:22:03.010
Another key to this is we're
just looking at the [INAUDIBLE]

00:22:03.010 --> 00:22:04.700
portal, the
application we built,

00:22:04.700 --> 00:22:07.420
as really just the
first consumer of this.

00:22:07.420 --> 00:22:09.580
This reporting
API that we built,

00:22:09.580 --> 00:22:12.900
we could stick Apigee in front
of this, given it an API key,

00:22:12.900 --> 00:22:15.340
throw it to a developer,
and the developer

00:22:15.340 --> 00:22:19.700
can get the same access
to the exact same data.

00:22:19.700 --> 00:22:21.400
You don't have to
play the scripts game.

00:22:21.400 --> 00:22:23.598
Everything is going through
a single channel, which

00:22:23.598 --> 00:22:26.140
guarantees that you're getting
the same beta, no matter what,

00:22:26.140 --> 00:22:29.440
from a single source.

00:22:29.440 --> 00:22:32.607
And I kind of alluded
to the performance--

00:22:32.607 --> 00:22:34.440
you can probably expect
that that's probably

00:22:34.440 --> 00:22:37.440
the biggest-- we've solved
most of the other challenges

00:22:37.440 --> 00:22:39.135
on our design goals.

00:22:39.135 --> 00:22:41.010
The two to three seconds
that you saw earlier

00:22:41.010 --> 00:22:44.268
was kind of what our product
team wanted to strive to.

00:22:44.268 --> 00:22:45.810
That's the biggest
challenge in this,

00:22:45.810 --> 00:22:47.290
but we've come a long way.

00:22:47.290 --> 00:22:48.540
So when we started this, what?

00:22:48.540 --> 00:22:51.690
18 months ago, roughly.

00:22:51.690 --> 00:22:54.510
Since then, last year,
they announced some custom

00:22:54.510 --> 00:22:56.340
partitioning for BigQuery.

00:22:56.340 --> 00:22:57.570
We've applied that.

00:22:57.570 --> 00:23:01.590
So putting in partitioning,
lowering the set of data

00:23:01.590 --> 00:23:03.630
that you're actually
querying against

00:23:03.630 --> 00:23:05.280
has improved time significantly.

00:23:05.280 --> 00:23:08.840
And we've actually implied
that on every single call.

00:23:08.840 --> 00:23:11.760
So every single
model that we have

00:23:11.760 --> 00:23:15.210
has a primary date that we have
set in BigQuery as the date

00:23:15.210 --> 00:23:16.200
partition.

00:23:16.200 --> 00:23:20.643
And we require the API calls
to pass in some kind of range.

00:23:20.643 --> 00:23:22.560
I can't let you go through
the whole data set.

00:23:22.560 --> 00:23:26.805
So I think the max you can
go back is maybe two years.

00:23:26.805 --> 00:23:27.430
It is a filter.

00:23:27.430 --> 00:23:30.270
You can say I can take seven
days, 30 days, yesterday,

00:23:30.270 --> 00:23:31.350
specific date.

00:23:31.350 --> 00:23:33.220
But we have to
constrain the data set.

00:23:33.220 --> 00:23:37.980
So you're not looking at the
whole lifetime of the data

00:23:37.980 --> 00:23:39.737
model.

00:23:39.737 --> 00:23:41.820
The other thing that we've
been experimenting with

00:23:41.820 --> 00:23:42.930
and has been--

00:23:42.930 --> 00:23:44.940
I think is going to have
some huge progress, is

00:23:44.940 --> 00:23:45.750
nested repeated.

00:23:49.237 --> 00:23:51.570
I talked about we have different
disparate data sources.

00:23:51.570 --> 00:23:53.237
Well, some of those
kind of fit together

00:23:53.237 --> 00:23:55.230
in kind of a
hierarchical pattern.

00:23:55.230 --> 00:23:59.010
You have this concept of a batch
when you're doing credit cards.

00:23:59.010 --> 00:24:02.340
Inside that, there may
be multiple transactions.

00:24:02.340 --> 00:24:04.290
Well, nested repeated
is a great way--

00:24:04.290 --> 00:24:07.390
instead of doing a
traditional RDBS join,

00:24:07.390 --> 00:24:09.360
you can actually put
those transactions

00:24:09.360 --> 00:24:15.050
as a column inside BigQuery,
as another nested table.

00:24:15.050 --> 00:24:18.540
And there's an un-nest feature,
which Looker speaks very well,

00:24:18.540 --> 00:24:20.700
and it's lightning quick.

00:24:20.700 --> 00:24:23.940
So we're migrating and we're
experimenting a little bit

00:24:23.940 --> 00:24:26.875
with moving our consumption
layer a little bit more

00:24:26.875 --> 00:24:29.250
to nested repeated and see if
we can get some performance

00:24:29.250 --> 00:24:29.792
improvements.

00:24:29.792 --> 00:24:34.010
So far, it looks
really promising.

00:24:34.010 --> 00:24:39.230
The last thing that I'll just
mention is wide and flat.

00:24:39.230 --> 00:24:41.550
The less joins you
have to do, the wider--

00:24:41.550 --> 00:24:44.000
it's columnar, so I
think most of our models

00:24:44.000 --> 00:24:47.680
are around 600 to 800 columns.

00:24:47.680 --> 00:24:51.220
And we're exposing all of
those in our consumption layer,

00:24:51.220 --> 00:24:54.250
where we've aggregated the
different data sets together.

00:24:54.250 --> 00:24:55.750
We go flat, and
wide, and disparate.

00:24:55.750 --> 00:24:56.260
That's fine.

00:24:56.260 --> 00:24:57.420
Leave them null.

00:24:57.420 --> 00:25:00.250
You only get what you ask
for back from BigQuery.

00:25:00.250 --> 00:25:02.920
So if you're only asking
for these eight columns,

00:25:02.920 --> 00:25:04.827
you're only going
to go through that--

00:25:04.827 --> 00:25:06.910
the data set's only going
to peruse eight columns.

00:25:06.910 --> 00:25:09.273
You can have that
as wide as you want.

00:25:09.273 --> 00:25:10.690
I'm sure there's
a physical limit,

00:25:10.690 --> 00:25:14.050
but I don't think
we've reached it.

00:25:14.050 --> 00:25:17.802
That helps when you're
looking at performance.

00:25:17.802 --> 00:25:19.760
Like, you can do these
aggregations on the fly,

00:25:19.760 --> 00:25:21.320
you can join them together.

00:25:21.320 --> 00:25:23.080
But what we found is
by flattening that

00:25:23.080 --> 00:25:25.630
out the consumption layer,
and having our Looker model

00:25:25.630 --> 00:25:27.760
point to the
flattened table, you

00:25:27.760 --> 00:25:30.790
get much better improvement.

00:25:30.790 --> 00:25:32.630
OK, so here's some
real world examples.

00:25:32.630 --> 00:25:36.700
This is just from
partitioning properly.

00:25:36.700 --> 00:25:38.260
You know, we started
out, these are

00:25:38.260 --> 00:25:41.570
five different kind of reports
that we've enabled through,

00:25:41.570 --> 00:25:43.390
that everyone gets.

00:25:43.390 --> 00:25:47.350
You can see in here, two
x is the worst we did.

00:25:47.350 --> 00:25:50.410
Just from partitioning,
some of them got to 10x.

00:25:50.410 --> 00:25:52.360
You can kind of see a
correlation between data

00:25:52.360 --> 00:25:53.290
processed and time.

00:25:55.810 --> 00:25:56.800
This is the key.

00:25:56.800 --> 00:25:59.415
First, I think, is
always apply a partition

00:25:59.415 --> 00:26:01.040
in every query you're
doing, especially

00:26:01.040 --> 00:26:04.060
if you're going to expose
this out in real time.

00:26:04.060 --> 00:26:06.760
I'm hoping the nested repeated
can get these even further.

00:26:06.760 --> 00:26:09.670
And we're probably averaging
in the five second range

00:26:09.670 --> 00:26:10.530
right now.

00:26:10.530 --> 00:26:12.530
To think about it, it's
still pretty impressive,

00:26:12.530 --> 00:26:15.760
when you're perusing
an entire petabyte data

00:26:15.760 --> 00:26:19.078
lake in real time.

00:26:19.078 --> 00:26:20.120
I think that's all I got.

00:26:20.120 --> 00:26:24.160
Mark's going to show you some
actual slides of the app.

00:26:24.160 --> 00:26:25.960
Thank you.

00:26:25.960 --> 00:26:27.100
MARK KUBIK: Thanks, JD.

